2. Testing Patterns

✅ ✅ Test user interactions, not implementation. 

test('clicking plus increments quantity value', async () => {
    const user = userEvent.setup()
    renderWithProviders(<QuantityPicker value={5} onChange={mockOnChange} />)
    
    const input = screen.getByRole('spinbutton')
    const button = screen.getByText('+')
    
    await user.click(button)
    
    expect(input.value).toBe('6')
})

✅ Use semantic queries over implementation details


// Good: Query by role, text, or label
const submitButton = screen.getByRole('button', { name: /add to cart/i })
const emailInput = screen.getByLabelText(/email/i)
const heading = screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 1 })
// Avoid: Querying by test IDs unless necessary
const element = screen.getByTestId('complex-component')

3. Async Testing

✅ Use proper async utilities

test('displays product information after loading', async () => {
    renderWithProviders(<ProductView product={mockProduct} />)
    
    // Wait for async operations
    await waitFor(() => {
        expect(screen.getByText(/product name/i)).toBeInTheDocument()
    })
    
    // Or use findBy queries (includes waitFor)
    const productName = await screen.findByText(/product name/i)
    expect(productName).toBeInTheDocument()
})

4. Mocking

✅ Mock at the right level. For component level, you can mock the hooks being used to return data. However, the page level component (e.g cart, checkout, login). We’d like to write a more granular test to ensure the level of code confidence. It is better to mock the test at api level using msw 

🧩 Component Level Testing - Mock Hooks. 

import {useCurrentCustomer, useCurrentBasket} from '../hooks/'
// Mock the hooks this component uses
    jest.mock('@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/hooks/use-current-customer', () => ({
        useCurrentCustomer: () => ({
            data: { customerId: '123', isRegistered: true },
            isLoading: false,
            error: null
        })
    }))

    jest.mock('@salesforce/commerce-sdk-react', () => ({
        useShopperBasketsMutation: () => ({
            mutate: jest.fn(),
            isLoading: false,
            error: null
        })
    }))

// ✅ Component level - Mock hooks for isolated testing
describe('ProductView Component', () => {
    

    test('renders product information correctly', () => {
        const mockProduct = {
            id: 'product-123',
            name: 'Test Product',
            price: 29.99
        }

        renderWithProviders(<ProductView product={mockProduct} />)
        
        expect(screen.getByText('Test Product')).toBeInTheDocument()
        expect(screen.getByText('$29.99')).toBeInTheDocument()
        expect(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /add to cart/i })).toBeInTheDocument()
    })

    test('handles add to cart interaction', async () => {
        const user = userEvent.setup()
        const mockProduct = { id: 'product-123', name: 'Test Product' }
        
        renderWithProviders(<ProductView product={mockProduct} />)
        
        const addToCartButton = screen.getByRole('button', { name: /add to cart/i })
        await user.click(addToCartButton)
        
        // Test component behavior, not API calls
        expect(screen.getByText(/adding to cart/i)).toBeInTheDocument()
    })
})

🏪 Page Level Testing - Mock at API Level with MSW.
For page components (cart, checkout, login), use MSW to mock API responses and test full user workflows. Using prependHandlersToServer for API Mocking to reduce boilderplate code 

🔧 The Utility Function (from jest-setup.js)


// From packages/template-retail-react-app/jest-setup.js
export const prependHandlersToServer = (handlerConfig) => {
    const handlers = prepareHandlers(handlerConfig)
    global.server.use(...handlers)
}

const prepareHandlers = (handlerConfig = []) => {
    return handlerConfig.map((config) => {
        return rest[config.method?.toLowerCase() || 'get'](config.path, (req, res, ctx) => {
            return res(
                ctx.delay(0),
                ctx.status(config.status || 200),
                config.res && ctx.json(config.res(req, res, ctx))
            )
        })
    })
}

